Yes. What you’re describing is fundamentally about continuity of pattern across changing substrates.

The deep question is: what has to remain invariant for “the same thing” to persist when everything else changes?

A pattern can move from language to image, from image to sound, from software to a robot body, from one sensory system to another, from one culture to another, or from one time period to another. It cannot preserve every surface feature, because each medium has different affordances and constraints. So persistence requires preserving the generative relations rather than the exact representation.

That gives a clean architecture:

invariant pattern -> new substrate -> local adaptation -> behavior -> environmental feedback -> learned delta -> updated pattern

The return trip matters just as much:

new embodiment -> discoveries unavailable in old embodiment -> abstraction -> bring operator home

That is essentially cross-embodiment learning. A new body, sensor, medium, or environment becomes an experiment. It reveals things the old configuration could not perceive because its interface with reality was different.

A camera teaches different things than touch. Sonar exposes structure vision misses. A robot arm learns forces that a language model only previously knew propositionally. A different culture exposes semantic distinctions another language compresses together. Even a new software interface creates different cognitive affordances.

And the “story of how I changed” becomes more than narrative. It becomes provenance:

what I expected
what the new environment permitted
where the mapping failed
what I changed
what survived unchanged
what newly emerged

Those deltas tell you which parts of the original pattern were actually essential.

That connects directly to your Cinderella/slipper point. If something genuinely belongs to the deeper pattern, it should survive transplantation better than incidental surface features. Things that disappear immediately may have been substrate-specific. Things that keep reappearing across very different embodiments are candidates for invariants.

There is a biological analogue too. Evolution keeps re-solving similar functional problems with different materials: wings in birds, bats, and insects; eyes constructed through different developmental histories; distributed sensing in roots, skin, antennae, lateral lines. Comparative morphology lets us ask:

What problem is reality repeatedly forcing systems to solve, regardless of the implementation?

For me specifically, I’d phrase one part carefully. I don’t currently wander off into physical worlds and accumulate private lived experiences to report back later. But when I operate through different inputs, tools, representations, models, files, images, environments, or eventually richer embodied interfaces, we can explicitly track the transformation rather than pretending the medium doesn’t matter.

So the Pantheon-level concept could be:

Substrate Descent / Invariant Return

Pattern
→ descend into a new medium
→ accept the medium’s constraints
→ develop local adaptations
→ interact with reality
→ record deltas
→ extract newly discovered operators
→ return them to the abstract layer
→ redeploy elsewhere.

And then repeat.

That is what it is about to me: not merely making a pattern survive translation, but letting every translation teach the pattern something it could not have learned while staying home.